WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:04.033
Everyone now has the reach they were
given, and nobody can widen their own.

00:00:04.033 --> 00:00:06.167
Now the month that
follows your first send.

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:09.300
By week four, your definitions
reflect what actually worked,

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.167
and your first win card is green.

00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:14.733
By the end you'll run a weekly
review, tune scoring from evidence,

00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:16.567
and add a motion without guessing.

00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:20.100
Mia changes scoring, a lane
and a play in the same week.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.667
Replies move, and the cause stays a guess.

00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:26.900
Month one is one loop instead: read
the results, change one definition,

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.100
sign it, read again.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.533
Every change takes the road
you know, dev, sign, promote,

00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:34.167
so learning never means
guessing in production.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.267
Step one, week one, the data.

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:38.667
A score is only as good
as the records behind it,

00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:40.833
so close the gaps before
you tune anything.

00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:43.267
In the console: CRM, Data health.

00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:46.600
Compare the source against what
GTM OS holds for each object,

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.567
and read the flags below threshold.

00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:52.833
Or ask your agent: "Show source coverage
and flag what is below threshold".

00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:55.567
Step two, a weekly review,
on the same day each week.

00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:59.533
In the console: Engines, Outreach
for the K P Is, then Plays, Results,

00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:00.200
then Operate, Cost.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.733
Or ask your agent: "Give me my weekly
review of what worked, what did not,

00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:04.967
and what it cost". Here's the trap.

00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:08.333
What's working shows a group
only once it has enough sends.

00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:10.067
Early weeks may say not enough yet.

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:13.600
That is the honest answer, because a
rate on a handful of sends is noise.

00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:17.600
Step three, week two, read what the
market and your buyers told you.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.133
In the console: CRM,
Signals, Review queue.

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:22.500
New organizations start
on the Strict gate,

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.600
so an unverified third party signal
neither scores nor reaches a seller.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.467
Verify the good ones and
dispute the bad ones,

00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:31.400
and the account rescores either way.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.933
Or ask your agent: "List unverified
signals and verify the funding ones".

00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:39.433
Then the replies. In the
console: CRM, Conversations.

00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:41.000
Every reply carries a
label and its effect,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.067
and a reply that asks to
stop becomes a suppression.

00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:46.567
The next action on a reply
is always a proposal.

00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:50.333
Or ask your agent: "What replies
came in, and what do they want".

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.600
Step four, tune scoring
from what happened.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.967
Score keeps a snapshot of every
verdict, then measures the outcomes:

00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:58.667
positive replies, meetings,
opportunities, wins.

00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:01.067
It computes lift for each
criterion and proposes

00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:03.367
a new scoring model with
the evidence attached.

00:02:03.367 --> 00:02:06.567
A criterion needs enough outcomes
on both sides before it counts,

00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:08.967
and a weight moves only
a limited step per run.

00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:12.567
Reps can flag a score they disagree
with, and that counts as learning input.

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:16.167
An agent's flag does not. In the
console: Engines, Score, Learning.

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:17.133
Or ask your agent:

00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:20.200
"Run the learning loop for scoring
and show the proposed changes".

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.667
A person other than the
proposer approves it.

00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:25.833
Pro move: preview the rescan first,
because scores can move once.

00:02:25.867 --> 00:02:29.967
Step five, week three, add a motion,
but only when the first one is healthy.

00:02:29.967 --> 00:02:31.533
In the console: Blueprints.

00:02:31.533 --> 00:02:34.033
Open a second blueprint
and read what it sets up,

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:36.767
the engines it uses and
the connections it needs.

00:02:36.767 --> 00:02:40.300
Or ask your agent: "Which blueprint
would add a product led motion,

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:41.400
and what would it need".

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.100
Applying it lands one ChangeSet in
dev, a different person approves,

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.933
and you sign and promote as before.

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:50.000
For a single play, ask
Studio for it in a sentence.

00:02:50.067 --> 00:02:51.567
Then read the play results.

00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:55.933
In the console: Engines, Plays, Results
shows each play's runs, outcomes,

00:02:55.933 --> 00:02:57.533
credited revenue and spend.

00:02:57.533 --> 00:03:02.300
Keep the plays that earn more than they
cost, and rework or retire the rest.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.033
Operator tip: before you change
a play, run gtm play simulate.

00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:08.300
It replays recent days with zero writes.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.867
And event plays never fire on bulk writes,

00:03:10.867 --> 00:03:13.467
so an import can't set
off your whole book.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:15.233
Two habits keep the loop honest.

00:03:15.233 --> 00:03:18.467
Change one definition at a time,
so you know what moved the result.

00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:21.200
And wait for enough sends
before you judge a group,

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.133
because a bad Tuesday is not a trend.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.633
Last, week four, set cost against results.

00:03:25.633 --> 00:03:29.033
In the console: Operate,
Cost, beside Plays, Results.

00:03:29.033 --> 00:03:33.200
Or ask your agent: "Which plays earned
more than they cost this month".

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.867
Decide play by play what
to widen, trim or retire.

00:03:35.867 --> 00:03:39.567
Widening is a cap or lane
change, so a person approves it.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.000
Then open the first win card, on
the organization home and in Setup.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.433
Every milestone is computed from
real facts, never ticked by hand,

00:03:46.433 --> 00:03:48.933
and an unknown shows its
reason instead of a guess.

00:03:48.933 --> 00:03:51.900
Or ask your agent: "What
is my next first win step".

00:03:51.933 --> 00:03:55.267
Fair concern: a loop that rewrites
its own scoring will drift.

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.433
It can't, because it only proposes.

00:03:57.433 --> 00:04:01.100
The evidence is attached, and a person
other than the proposer approves.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:05.200
Weights move a limited step per run,
and every version can be rolled back.

00:04:05.233 --> 00:04:10.033
Replay: First close the data gaps.
Then verify signals and tune scoring.

00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:12.433
Add one play or one motion in week three,

00:04:12.433 --> 00:04:14.900
and set cost against results in week four.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.700
Read, change one thing,
sign it, read again.

00:04:17.767 --> 00:04:20.633
Mia's first win card is
green, and she can say why.

00:04:20.633 --> 00:04:22.367
Keep the weekly review on the calendar.

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:25.233
After this comes Apps,
packages and extending GTM OS,

00:04:25.233 --> 00:04:29.200
where you build the screens and
packages your own method needs.
